Ported components: simple and general (d-GEV) scaling models, Gaussian /
Student-t / independence copulas with exponential and Matérn correlation
structures on log-durations, automatic initialisation, Wald (delta-method)
quantile intervals, Godambe/sandwich information, and the scaling-model
goodness-of-fit test. Please cite the original authors:

  Jalbert, J., Farmer, M., Gobeil, G., & Roy, P. (2024). Extremes.jl: Extreme
  Value Analysis in Julia. Journal of Statistical Software, 109(6), 1-35.
  https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v109.i06

  Mathivon, P., Genest, C., & Jalbert, J. (2024+). Joint modeling of annual
  precipitation maxima over several durations for the construction of
  intensity-duration-frequency curves.

  Paoli, A., Carreau, J., & Jalbert, J. (2024+). Statistical testing of
  scaling models for precipitation Intensity-Duration-Frequency curves.
  https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4838410

The bundled datasets `702S006.csv`, `6158731.csv` and `1108446.csv` are taken
from IDFCurves.jl (MIT), which retrieved them from Environment and Climate
Change Canada. The Québec station files under `tails/datasets/data/eccc_*`
are extracted from the ECCC Engineering Climate Datasets, IDF v3.40
(2025-12-05), https://collaboration.cmc.ec.gc.ca/cmc/climate/Engineer_Climate/IDF/
and are subject to the Open Government Licence – Canada.
